course: everyday
lesson: 411
title: Account swaps and passwords: the door key of your game
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Kai was halfway through a Fortnite match when a player called xX_GoldKing_Xx messaged him: «swap accounts? mine has way better skins, 40 of them, legit». Kai stared at his own skins — one slightly sad llama — and his thumb hovered over «yes». His cousin, reading over his shoulder with a mouthful of crisps, said «that's the oldest scam in the book, mate. You give him your login, he gives you nothing, and your llama's gone for ever.» Kai said no. The llama lived.
explain: Ollie gave his best friend his Roblox password «just to help with one level». The best friend was fine. The best friend's big brother was not: he found the password on the family tablet and spent every one of Ollie's coins on a hat shaped like a slice of toast. Priya's password was «priya123», which her whole class could have guessed, and one evening somebody did. Her dad helped her make a new one: «PurpleTromboneEatsCheese42», long, silly, and nothing to do with her name.
explain: Ruby got a message from someone calling himself «Official Game Moderator»: «verify your account or be BANNED, send password now». Ruby was scared, and that was the point of the message. Her mum explained that real game staff never ask for your password — not in a chat, not in an email, not ever — and they reported him. Mia got an email saying «your account will be deleted in 24 hours, click here», and her dad showed her the sender's address was a jumble of letters, not the game company at all.
explain: Some of the cast learned the quieter lessons. Theo's big brother logged in to «rank up» Theo's account, the game noticed the strange play and gave Theo a warning. Zara's password on a sticky note on her school book was read by a classmate who logged in as a joke. Noah forgot to log out at the library and his account posted «I LOVE BROCCOLI» in a chat. Jayden grumbled while setting up two-step login with his dad — and a month later, when a stranger tried to get in, the stranger failed. Leo gave a stranger his rare skin first because the stranger promised a «guaranteed» better one after; the better one never came.
fact: A password is like the key to your house: only you and your parents should have it.
fact: Real game staff and moderators never ask for your password.
fact: Offers to swap accounts or trade items with strangers outside the game are very often scams.
fact: A password made of your name and a few numbers is easy to guess.
fact: A long password made of several random words is much harder to guess and easy to remember.
fact: Two-step login sends a code to a parent's phone or email, so a stolen password alone is not enough to get in.
fact: Scam messages try to scare or rush you, so that you click or answer without thinking.
fact: Fake emails often come from strange addresses that only look a little like the real company.
fact: Letting someone else play on your account can get it flagged or banned by the game.
fact: If you must write a password down, keep it somewhere private at home, not on a school book or a tablet.
fact: On a shared computer, always log out when you finish.
fact: If you think someone has your password, tell a parent and change it straight away.
exercise: Why did the player want to swap accounts with Kai? | To take Kai's account and give nothing back | To give Kai a much better account for free, as a kind deed | Because he felt sorry for Kai's llama | Because the game asked him to swap
exercise: How did Kai feel when he saw the offer of 40 skins? | Tempted by all the shiny skins | Angry at the other player for showing off | Bored, he did not care about skins | Scared he would be banned for looking
exercise: What happened after Ollie gave his best friend his password? | The friend's brother spent all Ollie's coins | The friend finished the level and then forgot it | The game gave Ollie extra coins | Nothing at all, it was completely fine
exercise: Who should know your game password? | Only you and your parents | You and your best friend, if you trust each other | Your whole gaming squad and their mates | Anyone who says they are staff
exercise: Why was «priya123» a bad password? | Her name and easy numbers are simple to guess | It was far too long to type in quickly every time | It had no capital letters in it | Passwords should never have numbers
exercise: Which password is strongest? | PurpleTromboneEatsCheese42 | priya2016 | password1 | Priya-Is-Ten-Years-Old-2016
exercise: A «moderator» asks Ruby for her password. What is true? | Real staff never ask for your password | Moderators need it to check the accounts are safe | She must send it or be banned | She should send only half of it
exercise: Why did the message to Ruby say «or be BANNED»? | To scare her so she answered without thinking | Because she had broken a rule | Because moderators always write in capitals | To help her keep her account safe from hackers
exercise: How did Mia's dad know the email was fake? | The sender's address was a jumble, not the company | The email had a picture of the game at the top | The email said «please» | The email came on a Tuesday
exercise: What happened when Theo's brother ranked up Theo's account? | The game noticed strange play and warned Theo | Theo became famous at school for it | The brother got banned instead | Nothing, games cannot tell
exercise: Why did Zara's sticky note go wrong? | A classmate read it and logged in as a joke | The note fell off and got lost | The ink faded in the sun and she forgot the password | Her teacher took the book away
exercise: What is a wiser place for a written password? | Somewhere private at home | On the front of a school book | Inside a pencil case you take to school | On a sticker on the back of the tablet
exercise: What did Noah learn at the library? | Always log out on a shared computer | Never use a library computer ever again | Libraries keep and steal your passwords | Only use the library computers at night
exercise: Jayden thought two-step login was fussy. What happened after? | A stranger tried to get in and failed | It locked Jayden out of his own account for good | It made his game run slowly | His dad played on his account
exercise: How does two-step login protect you? | A stolen password alone is not enough to get in | It makes your password shorter and easier to remember | It hides your account from friends | It gives you free skins every week
exercise: Leo traded his rare skin for a «guaranteed» better one. What happened? | The better skin never arrived | He got two skins instead of one | The game doubled both skins | The trader felt bad and gave it back the next day
exercise: Which is a quiet kind of courage here? | Saying no to a friend who asks for your password | Sending your password to show you trust people | Swapping accounts to look brave | Clicking the link to see what happens
test: «bro swap accs, mine's stacked, 40 skins, trust me». best move? | Say no, swaps with strangers are usually scams | Swap, 40 skins is a great deal | Swap but only for one day | Ask him to send his password first
test: best mate: «gimme ur password i'll do the level for u». what do u say? | No thanks, I'll ask you to show me instead | Sure, best mates share everything | Only if he promises not to tell anyone else | Write it on his hand so he remembers
test: Ruby's DM: «OFFICIAL MOD. send password or ur BANNED in 10 min!!» What is this? | A scam, real staff never ask for passwords | A real warning she must obey within ten minutes | A test that she passes by answering | A joke from the game's makers
test: email: «ur account deleted in 24h unless u click». Mia's next move? | Show a grown-up before clicking anything | Click fast before the time runs out | Reply asking if it is real | Forward it to all her friends to warn them
test: «my password's my dog's name, nobody knows my dog lol». Safe? | No, friends and classmates can easily guess it | Yes, dogs are secret | Yes, if the dog is new and nobody's met it yet | Only if the dog is big
test: Theo's brother: «I'll rank you up, give us ur login». What's the risk? | The game may flag or ban the account | The rank will be too high to enjoy | Theo will get a free skin | Nothing, brothers are allowed to share
test: Zara: «wrote my password on my book so I won't forget, smart innit?» Best reply? | Not smart, anyone who sees the book can read it | Very smart, school books are private | Smart, if she writes it really small in the corner | Only smart if it's in pencil
test: Noah's account posts «I LOVE BROCCOLI» while he's at home. What most likely happened? | He stayed logged in on a shared computer | The game made a mistake and posted it | His broccoli hacked him | The library deleted his account
test: Jayden: «two-step is sooo boring, dad». What's true? | It stops strangers even if they get the password | It is only useful for grown-ups with bank accounts | It makes the game easier to hack | It is a kind of scam itself
test: trader: «give me ur rare skin first, I'll send the better one after, guaranteed». Wise? | No, trades with strangers outside the game are where tricks happen | Yes, «guaranteed» means it's safe | Yes, if he types it in capitals | Only if he says please
test: Priya finds out someone logged in to her account. First thing? | Tell a parent and change the password | Make the same password but a bit longer | Keep quiet and hope it stops on its own | Log in to their account back to get even
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a pupil ought not to disclose the password of a game account to any party other than a parent or guardian. | Any party possessing the password may use or appropriate the account | Disclosure is permissible solely to persons of equal age, such as classmates | Passwords lose their validity once spoken aloud | Disclosure is inadvisable solely in the case of strangers
exam: Whereas a message purporting to originate from a game moderator demands the immediate surrender of a password under threat of suspension, select the response consistent with established guidance. | The message ought to be treated as fraudulent and reported | The password ought to be furnished without delay to avert suspension | The password ought to be furnished in part only | The message ought to be forwarded to acquaintances
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises two-step authentication? | It renders a password insufficient on its own to obtain access | It renders a password unnecessary in all circumstances whatsoever | It permits third parties to share an account lawfully | It shortens the period for which a password is valid
exam: Select the response most consistent with the established facts concerning the proposed exchange of accounts with an unknown player. | Such proposals are frequently fraudulent and ought to be declined | Such proposals are invariably advantageous to the pupil | Such proposals are lawful only where the skins are numerous | Such proposals ought to be accepted upon receipt of a written guarantee
